          { name: "Young Professional Economic Opportunity and Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 28.2 median age and 72.6% college attainment signal a constituency of educated young adults evaluating on whether the urban environment is delivering professional opportunity, reasonable cost of living, public safety, and access to services that justify remaining in the city. Student debt relief, workforce development, transit investment, and neighborhood safety are the material governance issues that move this constituency more than partisan affiliation." },
          { name: "Subconstituency Service to Asian, Hispanic, and Black Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The 12.22% Asian community and combined ~11% Hispanic and Black population are meaningful but not dominant in a 72% white plurality district. In cities where demographic change is visible, these communities can rapidly mobilize electoral participation when they perceive the incumbent is delivering for them, or conversely can depress turnout when they feel neglected. Specific constituent attention—not generic multicultural messaging—is required to prevent this subconstituency work from becoming invisible." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as a Referendum on Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% 2024 result reflects an absence of Republican opposition in a heavily Democratic district, not voter enthusiasm for the incumbent's record or stability in the constituency's partisan alignment. Young renters, college-educated professionals, and urban voters of color have demonstrated the capacity to reduce turnout or support challengers when material conditions deteriorate or when they perceive incumbent indifference to their concerns. Interpreting the unopposed margin as a durable base is a strategic miscalculation." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Abstract Partisan Messaging Over Concrete Delivery Narratives", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency this young, educated, and urban does not respond durably to generic Democratic messaging or appeals to vote for the party—it responds to specific accounts of policy wins that improve housing, transit, public safety, or professional opportunity. An incumbent who campaigns on partisan identity rather than demonstrating concrete delivery on the issues that move this constituency will find that loyalty insufficient to hold turnout if opposition emerges or if voter enthusiasm for the incumbent's record wanes." }
