          { name: "Cultural and Social Moderation Expectations", tag: "careful", why: "College-educated suburban voters at high income levels, particularly women and younger professionals, increasingly evaluate candidates on whether their party's cultural positioning aligns with moderate, inclusive values — abortion access, LGBTQ+ rights, and racial equity being the most common swing factors. A district at 57-43 Republican with 61% college education suggests some portion of affluent voters are uncomfortable with their party's cultural agenda; candidates must navigate this carefully by acknowledging that diverse viewpoints exist within the district and that good governance serves all constituents respectfully." },
          { name: "Treating 57% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this affluent, this educated, and this close to 50-50, a 57% margin is not a durable floor — it is a fragile coalition held together by the incumbent's specific positioning and the opposition's failures to credibly compete for swing voters. Any candidate who assumes that 57% insulates them from active constituent engagement with the college-educated swing bloc will find that assumption tested quickly; affluent educated voters have the information access and political confidence to shift if they lose faith in their representative's competence or values." },
          { name: "Ignoring or Alienating the 43% Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 43% voted against the incumbent, a significant portion of the constituency is actively in play — likely concentrated among college-educated professionals concerned about social issues, education adequacy, or fiscal fairness. Any candidate who campaigns solely to consolidate the existing 57% without addressing what generated that 43% opposition is leaving a swing constituency unmobilized and available for the opposition to persuade; in a district this competitive and educated, the margin belongs to whoever best understands and addresses both constituencies' actual concerns." }
