          { name: "Treating 56% as an Insurmountable Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A ten-point margin in a district whose demographics would predict a 60%+ Republican result represents a gap between expected and actual performance — a signal that the current Democratic advantage is contingent on specific factors (incumbent credibility, constituent satisfaction with fiscal governance, professional-class alignment) rather than a structural demographic ceiling. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 56% represents a safe hold is ignoring the fact that a five-point swing brings the race to statistical dead-even in a district with this profile. A challenger who can credibly argue that the incumbent is misaligned with the district's actual governance priorities has a viable mathematical path to victory." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Governance Competence", tag: "avoid", why: "PA-HD-29's high income, education level, and homeownership rate describe a constituency that evaluates candidates on perceived competence and alignment with professional-class values rather than partisan tribe loyalty. A campaign that leads with partisan messaging, ideological positioning, or appeals to partisan base enthusiasm rather than specific, concrete governance achievements in the district will underperform with the actual decision-making criteria of affluent professionals who have options and make voting decisions based on whether government is working for them. This is a constituency that can be persuaded, but only through competence-based and results-based argumentation." }
