          { name: "Black Community Representation & Racial Equity in City Services", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 33.6% Black plurality makes the Black community the largest single demographic group in the district, yet representation on criminal justice, police accountability, and equitable allocation of municipal services is often the constituency concern most easily neglected in favor of generic Democratic messaging. A district where one-third of constituents are Black voters requires that representation be active and measurable on these issues, not assumed; the unopposed outcome means this accountability has not been publicly tested, creating vulnerability to a challenger who makes specific commitments on these issues." },
          { name: "Economic Vulnerability in an Unopposed Seat", tag: "careful", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 is an electoral advantage that can mask real constituent discontent or unmet needs that a future challenger could activate. With 49.8% renters, 43.3% college-educated, and a $58,719 income that sits above but not well above the national median, this is a working-class to lower-middle-class district where inflation, housing costs, and student debt are real pressures that no incumbent has had to defend against publicly. The 100-0 result provides no information about whether constituent satisfaction is genuine or whether opposition simply did not materialize due to field neglect rather than incumbent strength." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Result as Evidence of Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed outcomes in mid-term elections frequently reflect field weakness or timing rather than genuine incumbent strength, and the next competitive cycle — particularly if a credible challenger emerges on housing, racial equity, or economic security — can reveal that the current margin is less stable than it appears. An incumbent who assumes the 100-0 result is evidence of a consolidated base rather than an absence of opposition will be unprepared when a challenger emerges around tenant rights, police accountability, or economic opportunity for young adults." },
          { name: "Neglecting Specific Constituent Work in Favor of Partisan Certainty", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed result can encourage an incumbent to rely on partisan loyalty and generic Democratic messaging rather than building relationships on the specific material concerns of renters, the Black community, and working families; this approach leaves the seat vulnerable to a challenger who addresses these concerns with specificity and builds relationships through retail politics. A district with a 49.8% renter rate, 33.6% Black plurality, and mixed education and income profile requires active constituent engagement, not assumption that Democratic partisanship alone will hold the seat in a more competitive environment." }
