          { name: "Non-College Economic Messaging", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 65.8% non-college-educated majority contradicts the affluence signals and requires a distinct economic messaging frame than a comparable college-educated district would. Small-business viability, skilled-trades workforce stability, healthcare worker compensation and working conditions, and middle-management job security are the economic vectors that matter for a non-credentialed majority at this income level. A candidate who speaks to these constituencies' specific economic needs rather than defaulting to college-class economic narratives builds credibility with the district's actual demographic composition." },
          { name: "The Unexplained Gap Between 62% and Expected 70%+", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this affluent (83K median income), this homeowner-dominant (82%), and this white (90%), a Republican should expect 68-72% as a floor. The 62% result represents approximately 4-5 percentage points of underperformance relative to demographic expectation — meaningful in a district this size and suggesting either swing-voter persuadability or active discouragement with the incumbent or party. This gap is either a warning signal of erosion or an opportunity for a challenger to expand; either way, understanding what drives it is more strategically valuable than assuming the current 62% is durable." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Culture-War Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A non-college-educated, older, homeowner-dominant constituency at $83K income evaluates candidates primarily on material economic security and effective governance, not on cultural positioning. Campaigns that overweight culture-war issues relative to healthcare costs, property tax management, and small-business viability are misaligned with the actual priority hierarchy of HD-39 voters and leave room for an economically-focused challenger to gain ground by answering the material concerns the incumbent is ignoring." },
          { name: "Treating HD-39 as Safely Republican Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 38% Democratic share in an affluent, homeowner-dominant district is not trivial; it represents either a credible challenger's support or genuine swing capacity waiting to be mobilized. A candidate who assumes the current 62-38 result is a stable floor and directs limited resources elsewhere is leaving 38% of the district unaddressed and creating an opening for a challenger with specific economic messaging to reframe the race. The gap between current performance and demographic expectation makes this a seat that requires active persuasion, not passive confidence in the existing margin." }
