          { name: "Culture War Positioning and Identity-Based Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent suburban professionals with high education levels are least responsive to abstract cultural or identity-based conservative messaging and most responsive to concrete governance performance on education, fiscal management, and local services; a representative whose primary focus is culture war positioning rather than constituent service will hemorrhage support to Democratic challengers who offer school funding and property tax relief instead. The 46% Democratic share in a district of this demographic profile is a warning that cultural conservatism alone does not win or hold affluent suburban seats; competence and service delivery do." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe Majority in a High-Education District", tag: "avoid", why: "In Pennsylvania's affluent suburbs, a 54-46 result is not a solid floor—it is a working margin that requires active defense on the issues that matter most to college-educated homeowners: education, fiscal responsibility, and constituent service. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 54% represents a comfortable hold in a district where 51.7% are college-educated and 79% are homeowners will miscalculate the persuadability of this constituency; the underperfromance relative to comparable high-income districts is a signal that the current Republican margin is contingent on continued competent governing, not secured by demographic inevitability." }
