          { name: "Cost of Living & Property Values", tag: "lean-into", why: "The median household income of $59,391 combined with a 31.7% renter rate signals affordability pressures and economic vulnerability among a significant segment. Property tax burden and housing cost growth are likely top-of-mind concerns for both renters and homeowners in a district where income growth may not be keeping pace with housing market dynamics. Messaging that addresses kitchen-table affordability—property tax relief, rent stability, or wage growth alignment—resonates directly with the economic reality of nearly one-third of households." },
          { name: "Infrastructure & Local Services", tag: "lean-into", why: "A median age of 39.5 in a 90.41% white, predominantly suburban district indicates stable, family-oriented households with established stakes in community infrastructure. Roads, schools, utilities, and public safety are not abstract policy; they are direct drivers of property values and quality of life in this demographic cohort. Concrete delivery on potholes, school funding, and emergency response capacity builds trust and demonstrates stewardship in a district where local institutions are central to voter identity." },
          { name: "Educational Access & Skills Training", tag: "careful", why: "With 30% college-educated adults, the district sits at a pivot point: higher education is present but not dominant, and a 70% non-college-educated majority may feel left behind by credentialism or workforce skills mismatches. Messaging must acknowledge value in both traditional and vocational pathways without inadvertently signaling that non-degree holders are second-tier. Support for community colleges, apprenticeships, and technical training can bridge this divide, but framing matters—avoid language that implies college is the only pathway to dignity or security." },
          { name: "Cultural or Identity-Based Divisiveness", tag: "avoid", why: "The district is 90.41% white with modest representation from other groups (Black 3.06%, Hispanic 2.08%, Asian 0.97%, and ~3.48% other including American Indian/Alaska Native). Culture-war messaging or appeals rooted in racial or ethnic resentment are both unnecessary and counterproductive; they alienate the small but real minority populations and distract from the economic and local service issues that unite the base. Strength lies in universalist framing on jobs, schools, and infrastructure." },
          { name: "Partisan Partisan Nationalization & Hyperpartisanship", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed Republican victory (100% Rep, 0% Dem) reflects either dominant partisan alignment or Democratic organizational collapse—either way, voters in this district are not engaged in a close fight and do not reward scorched-earth partisan rhetoric. Lean on local accomplishment, constituent service, and bipartisan problem-solving; national culture-war grievances muddy the message in a district where local competence and stability are the real currency. Aggressive partisanship reads as noise when the opposition is absent." }
