        memoHeadline: "A stable, homeowner-majority suburban district with real affordability pressure—hold through local delivery and avoid culture-war distraction.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "This is a solidly conservative suburban district where the electorate is older (median 39.5), white (90.41%), and rooted—but not wealthy. A $59,391 median household income places this district firmly in the working-to-middle-class range, not an affluent enclave. Nearly one-third are renters, meaning housing stability and cost management are live concerns for a significant minority even as homeownership remains the norm. The 2024 unopposed result signals strong Republican consolidation, but that advantage is fragile if the underlying economic covenant—affordable housing, job security, accessible services—erodes. Strategic strength here comes not from partisan activation but from demonstrating competent, tangible stewardship on the issues that affect household budgets and daily life.",
          "The moderate college-education rate (30%) reinforces that this is a practical, service-oriented constituency, not an ideological one. Voters here are skeptical of abstract theory but responsive to results: fixed roads, funded schools, responsive police, and support for wage-earning pathways matter more than rhetorical purity. The demographic homogeneity (90%+ white) creates a temptation for identity-based messaging, but that is a trap—it is unnecessary, alienates the real minority populations present, and crowds out the local economic message that actually holds this seat. Victory here turns on proving that government works for working families: that property taxes are managed, that schools deliver, that infrastructure is maintained, and that job prospects are improving or at least stable. The absence of a Democratic opponent in 2024 should not breed complacency; it should sharpen focus on delivering the concrete wins that keep voters from looking elsewhere."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with kitchen-table economics: property tax reform, rent stabilization, and wage opportunity. The $59k median income is not wealthy; affordability is the binding constraint.",
          "Invest heavily in constituent service and local problem-solving (potholes, permit delays, school accountability). Local delivery builds trust in a district where partisan alternatives are not active.",
          "Emphasize inclusive workforce pathways—apprenticeships, trade schools, community colleges—alongside traditional degree programs. Do not cede skills and opportunity messaging to educational elitism.",
          "Avoid culture-war framing entirely. It is unnecessary in a 90%+ white district, alienates small but real minority populations, and distracts from the local governance story that wins elections here."
        ]
