          { name: "Dismissing the 61% Result as Anomalous or Incumbent-Specific", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% Democratic margin in a 95% white, 73.9% homeowner, $61,302-income district is not a fluke or a referendum on a single popular candidate — it reflects constituent alignment with the incumbent's approach to economic security and governance. Any challenger who treats this as a temporary outcome that will reverse with partisan messaging or demographic assumptions rather than understanding and credibly addressing the specific concerns that generated it will enter the general election strategically blind to what actually motivates this electorate." },
          { name: "Pivoting to Abstract Partisan or Social Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The 19.5% college-attainment rate and working-class, homeowner-majority composition indicate a constituency more responsive to concrete policy proposals affecting their material circumstances than to abstract ideological positioning. A challenger who leads with partisan identity messaging, cultural issues, or ideological consistency rather than specific answers to property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and job security will likely find that messaging resonates poorly with a district that has just decisively elected a Democrat on what appears to be economic-security grounds." }
