          { name: "Education Quality and School Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district where 50.6% of adults hold college degrees and median household income exceeds $118,500, K-12 education is evaluated as both a public good and a competitive advantage for children's future positioning. Voters at this income and education level are highly attentive to curriculum rigor, standardized test performance, teacher quality, and whether education dollars are producing measurable outcomes. Any incumbent Democrat must lead with concrete education achievements — funding increases translated into classroom impact, student outcome metrics, and evidence-based policy — rather than assuming that progressive values are sufficient to hold this constituency's support on schools." },
          { name: "Fiscal Responsibility and Tax Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "Upper-middle-class and affluent voters often hold paradoxical tax politics: they want strong public services but demand proof that spending is efficient, waste is minimized, and tax burden is justified by performance. At $118,529 median income with substantial property values, this constituency is acutely aware of tax burden and skeptical of spending claims not backed by measurable results. Leading on fiscal management, performance metrics for public services, and specific waste reduction builds credibility with the 40% opposition and consolidates the Democratic base among voters who value both government effectiveness and fiscal discipline." },
          { name: "Renter Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 20.4% renter rate in a high-income district indicates a cohort of younger professionals, service workers, and potentially displaced households whose economic position is structurally less secure than the homeowner majority. Housing affordability, zoning reform, and wage growth for professional-class workers are material concerns for renters in expensive housing markets, and this group can become a swing constituency if they conclude the incumbent is prioritizing homeowner interests over their economic mobility. Specific housing policy engagement with renters — rather than treating them as incorporated into the general 'progressive housing' narrative — is necessary to maintain support." },
          { name: "Dismissing Property Tax and Fiscal Concerns as 'Anti-Government'", tag: "avoid", why: "In high-income, educated districts, fiscal conservatism often reflects genuine skepticism about government efficiency rather than ideological opposition to public services. Characterizing upper-middle-class concern about property taxes and spending efficiency as hostile to public sector values will alienate swing voters in the 40% opposition who are sophisticated enough to distinguish between wanting effective government and being frustrated with government waste. This framing concedes the district's persuadable center." },
          { name: "Treating Black and Asian Communities as Automatically Incorporated into the 'Educated Professional' Narrative", tag: "avoid", why: "At 10.26% Black and 6.97% Asian, these are meaningful minority communities with specific economic mobility, healthcare access, and professional advancement concerns that can be invisible in high-income district politics focused on homeowner property values. Minority communities in affluent districts often become swing voters when they conclude they are taken for granted by the incumbent, and assuming that minority voters in a wealthy, educated district are automatically consolidated behind the Democratic baseline is a vulnerability that can be exploited by credible outreach from opponents. Active constituent engagement and specific policy responsiveness are necessary." }
