          { name: "Young Adult Housing & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.0 median age — the youngest in most comparable districts — combined with a 30.5% renter rate signals a significant young adult constituency facing real housing affordability constraints and economic mobility concerns. At $79,809 household income with 42.6% college-educated adults, the district contains both established professionals and younger college-educated adults entering the housing market; for renters in this demographic composition, housing cost burden and the pathway to homeownership are direct quality-of-life pressures. A legislative agenda addressing housing affordability, student debt management, and first-time homebuyer assistance resonates with a constituency that is educationally positioned but economically constrained by housing markets and educational debt." },
          { name: "The 46% Opposition & Swing Voter Composition", tag: "careful", why: "A 54-46 Democratic win is a narrow margin in what would be a swing seat in a less blue state; understanding whether the 46% represents a consolidating Republican base or a genuinely persuadable constituency is critical. With 82.43% white, 42.6% college-educated, and $79,809 income, the opposition includes college-educated white professionals who may be evaluating on economics, education policy, or governance competence rather than inherited partisan loyalty. The combination of a 30.0 median age and 30.5% renter rate means younger swing voters are evaluating whether current governance is delivering on housing, education, and economic opportunity — not abstract partisan positioning." },
          { name: "Treating College Education as a Partisan Issue Rather Than an Economic Necessity", tag: "avoid", why: "With 42.6% college-educated adults, HD-82 is a constituency that views higher education as the foundation of economic security and community stability — not as a partisan talking point. Any candidate who frames education funding or higher education policy as a partisan wedge rather than a shared community investment will find this messaging actively rejected by a plurality for whom educational access is a material household concern. The college-educated professionals and parents in this district need solutions to education financing and workforce alignment, not culture-war rhetoric." },
          { name: "Dismissing Housing Affordability as a 'Market Issue' Rather Than a Governance Problem", tag: "avoid", why: "A 30.0 median age with 30.5% renter rate in a $79,809 household-income district creates a constituency directly experiencing housing cost burden — not an abstract policy debate. Any candidate whose response to housing affordability is libertarian rhetoric about 'letting the market work' or dismissal of affordability concerns as 'coastal elite problems' misreads the material reality of young renters in this district who are college-educated, earning above-median income, and still unable to access homeownership. This constituency evaluates on concrete solutions: zoning reform, development incentives, first-time homebuyer programs — not ideological positioning." }
