          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $58,456 median income with 38.8% renters and 38.7-year median age, HD-83 is a working-class constituency where healthcare costs, housing affordability, and wage growth directly determine quality of life and electoral loyalty. A representative who delivers on specific cost-of-living concerns — property tax management, healthcare access and affordability, rental housing regulation, or local job development — builds the material constituent relationship that survives partisan swings. The unopposed 2024 victory should not be read as endorsement; it should be read as opportunity to demonstrate real economic benefit before a funded opposition challenger enters the race." },
          { name: "Local Employment & Economic Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with 24.2% college attainment and a working-class base, local job availability and wages are direct determinants of whether families stay in the community or seek opportunity elsewhere. State legislative priorities around workforce development, small business support, and retention of employers in the district are the economic development channels a representative controls. For a constituency with median income below state average and significant renter concentration, visible legislative action on keeping and attracting jobs that pay sustainable wages is the most credible signal that government is serving their interests." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability for Working-Class Families", tag: "careful", why: "A working-class, aging district (38.7 median age) at $58,456 income is precisely the population for which healthcare costs and access drive financial stress and political evaluation. Whether the incumbent has fought for Medicaid expansion, supported rural healthcare facility viability, or addressed prescription drug costs is the specific healthcare record that working-class constituents will evaluate when a challenger raises these issues. The district's healthcare politics are not ideologically settled; they are practically driven by whether families can access care and afford it." },
          { name: "Partisan Positioning Divorced from Material Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A working-class base at $58,456 income, with 38.8% renters and genuine economic pressure, is not ideologically aligned — it is materially aligned with whichever candidate and party offers real solutions to cost-of-living concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns on partisan messaging without concrete legislative delivery on the economic issues this constituency faces is vulnerable to a challenger who offers material solutions, regardless of the opposition candidate's partisan label. The 2024 unopposed victory should not be misread as ideological consolidation." },
          { name: "Taking the Unopposed Victory as Strategic Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result in a district with working-class economic stress, significant renter concentration, and below-average income is not a mandate — it is a moment of Democratic resource scarcity that will not last if the seat becomes competitive. Any incumbent who treats this seat as safe and does not actively build genuine constituent relationships around the material concerns that matter to $58,456-income renters and homeowners will find a funded Democratic challenger in 2026 or beyond with a receptive audience for economic messaging. The absence of 2024 opposition is tactical, not strategic." }
