          { name: "Education Funding and School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.6% college attainment — substantially above Pennsylvania's state average — nearly half of HD-88's adult population is actively invested in education as a household priority. K-12 funding quality directly affects property values and family well-being in affluent suburban districts, and education policy is a primary decision driver for college-educated parents. In a district where 55-45 splits educated voters, the candidate who leads on education funding and school investment captures the constituency most likely to shift between parties based on concrete policy performance." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "While $89,608 income provides healthcare purchasing power, healthcare access and cost are material concerns across suburban households — particularly for the 29% renter population and older homeowners on fixed incomes. Healthcare messaging focused on provider networks, prescription drug costs, and insurance affordability resonates across affluent constituencies without partisan framing and directly addresses household economic concerns in an expensive suburban market." },
          { name: "Young Renter and Working-Parent Economics", tag: "careful", why: "The 29.1% renter population in an affluent district represents young professionals, service-sector workers, and multi-generational households whose economic concerns diverge significantly from the homeowner majority. Childcare affordability, student loan burdens, and housing costs in expensive suburban markets are concrete economic constraints for this constituency, and a candidate who addresses these specifics rather than assuming homeowner-majority priorities can mobilize renter turnout. At 55-45, the renter and younger-adult vote is the swing constituency most capable of shifting the outcome." },
          { name: "Assuming 55% Represents a Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 split in a district with 43.6% college attainment is not a comfortable Republican hold — it is evidence of a genuinely contested constituency where educated voters are split. The post-2016 realignment in affluent suburban districts has been driven by college-educated voters' shift away from Republican positioning on education, healthcare, and social issues. Treating 55% as a stable floor rather than a narrow margin held by homeowner consolidation misreads the underlying electoral dynamics of HD-88." },
          { name: "Framing Opposition as Non-Affluent or Marginal", tag: "avoid", why: "The 45% opposition in HD-88 is not a coalition of low-income or non-college voters — it is primarily college-educated, affluent, suburban voters whose policy priorities diverge from Delozier on education, healthcare, and social issues. Any messaging strategy that implicitly positions the opposition as outside the district's mainstream affluent character misreads who is actually voting against the incumbent and alienates persuadable educated voters who may be considering the alternative." }
