          { name: "Latino & Black Coalition Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "Together, Latino (29.19%) and Black (20.09%) voters comprise 49.28% of the district—nearly half—and their electoral turnout and candidate preference are the difference between a 59-41 Democratic hold and a competitive race. These communities contain voters who evaluate on economic results and community investment, not inherited partisan affiliation. Hill-Evans must demonstrate specific legislative attention to Latino workforce issues (wage protection, labor standards, immigrant integration), Black community economic opportunity (small business access, workforce development, anti-discrimination enforcement), and shared working-class concerns (healthcare, housing, education funding) with equal specificity and resource allocation." },
          { name: "Young Constituency Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 34-year median age creates a constituency with different life-stage concerns—student debt, first-time homeownership, career mobility—than older working-class voters. This younger cohort is demographically diverse and more economically insecure than their parents' generation at the same age. If Democratic governance is perceived as offering sympathy without concrete solutions on housing affordability or wage growth, younger workers across all demographic groups can become persuadable by a Republican economic messaging that emphasizes job creation and cost control, even if that messaging is ultimately hollow." },
          { name: "Treating the 59% as a Secure Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "An 18-point margin is substantial, but it rests entirely on delivering for a working-class majority-minority constituency whose baseline economic condition is precarious. Any incumbent governance agenda that is perceived as failing on housing, wages, or job quality will erode the Latino and Black voter turnout or candidate preference that generated the 59%, and a 59-41 hold can quickly become a genuinely competitive race. The margin is not the reflection of stable partisan sorting; it is the reflection of current voter satisfaction with Democratic responsiveness to material concerns." },
          { name: "Allowing the Majority-Minority Demographic Story to Substitute for Economic Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "Cultural and social messaging on democratic rights, equity, and community respect are necessary elements of Democratic coalition-building, but in PA-HD-95 they are not sufficient to hold the seat if economic governance appears to be failing. An incumbent or challenger who relies on Latino and Black voter support through identity-affirming messaging while offering no specific legislative remedies for housing costs, wage growth, or job quality will find the coalition fragmenting when voters experience their paychecks not covering rent or their children unable to afford college. The demographic composition of the district creates Democratic advantage, but that advantage must be translated into material economic results." }
