          { name: "Hispanic Population Growth and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.58% Hispanic population represents a meaningful and likely-growing demographic segment that has materially strengthened Democratic performance in similar suburban Pennsylvania districts. This constituency's primary concerns center on wage growth, housing affordability, and economic mobility — not abstract ideology. Generic Democratic messaging on opportunity is insufficient; specific legislative results or candidate commitment to Hispanic-specific economic priorities (construction wages, small-business lending, housing affordability) are required to consolidate and grow this coalition. Neglecting active Hispanic constituent service and economic messaging in a district where this population is growing risks allowing Republican recruitment to achieve modest gains that compress the overall margin." },
          { name: "Renter and Young-Worker Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 38.8% renter rate signals a secondary constituency of younger professionals and service-sector workers whose economic concerns differ from homeowners' — housing affordability, wage growth, and job availability are their primary quality-of-life issues. This constituency has voted Democratic in PA-HD-96, but its satisfaction is conditional on perceiving tangible economic improvement, not partisan affiliation alone. In a district where inflation and cost-of-living pressures are real material constraints on household budgets, this constituency can become vulnerable to any challenger offering concrete wage or housing-affordability commitments. Understanding and serving this renter constituency requires economic messaging distinct from homeowner focus and specific policy results on housing and wage issues." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Safe Majority Requiring No Active Defense", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in a swing state like Pennsylvania is a solid margin but not a permissible floor for complacency. The constituency mix — college-educated homeowners, Hispanic workers, renters, and younger professionals — requires active, ongoing constituent service to consolidate. Any incumbent who operates under the assumption that 60% is a baseline floor risk allowing attrition among any of the three primary constituency segments. In Pennsylvania's recent electoral history, similar margins have compressed in both directions; the district requires active defense, not passive confidence." },
          { name: "Positioning Education and Healthcare as 'Special Interests' Rather Than Core Constituent Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 46.4% college-educated district with strong commitment to public systems, framing education funding or healthcare access as special-interest demands rather than core constituent priorities is rhetorically and strategically dangerous. The college-educated homeowner majority and the working-class renter constituency share interest in public education and healthcare quality, even if their specific priorities differ. Any messaging that positions teachers' unions or healthcare advocates as competing interests rather than representing authentic constituent needs will alienate the Democratic base while failing to persuade swing voters who evaluate on material outcomes." }
