          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Cost of Living", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $62,392 median household income with only 18.3% college-educated adults, PA-HD-107 is a working-class district where economic pressure is material and immediate. Healthcare costs, property tax burden, and wage stagnation relative to cost of living are the primary financial concerns for a constituency without educational credentials that typically unlock income mobility. An incumbent running unopposed has no electoral pressure to address these concerns, creating a latent vulnerability if a challenger emerges who speaks directly to the economic anxiety of non-college-educated working families." },
          { name: "Aging Community Infrastructure & Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 44.7-year median age combined with the $62,392 income and low college-attainment rate signals a community where older adults are aging in place with fixed incomes and healthcare systems serving a population that skews toward chronic disease management. Rural hospital viability, Medicare sustainability, and access to specialists in a geographically dispersed region are governance concerns that directly affect household financial security and quality of life. A representative who can demonstrate concrete delivery on healthcare access and cost management builds durable constituent loyalty that transcends partisan identity in aging communities." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory & Hidden Opposition Vulnerabilities", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% Republican result with no Democratic opposition is arithmetically stable but strategically dangerous — it provides zero information about whether the remaining ~35-40% of voters who typically support Democrats in statewide elections are genuinely absent from this district or simply unmobilized and unrepresented. An incumbent who has not faced opposition has not been tested on specific constituent grievances, and the absence of electoral pressure can create blind spots about what a credible challenger could activate. Any strategic planning must account for the possibility that 2026 or 2028 produces a competitive challenger with resources, particularly if economic conditions deteriorate or healthcare access declines in visible ways." },
          { name: "Anti-Rural Sentiment or Regional Dismissal Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class, non-college-educated rural constituencies are responsive to messaging that validates their economic and social concerns as legitimate, but highly resistant to any implication that their region is backward, dependent, or less worthy of investment than urban areas. Language that positions rural Pennsylvania as a problem to be managed rather than a community deserving of direct economic development investment will activate latent opposition from a constituency that, while currently Republican, is not immune to candidate-specific defection." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Ideology Divorced from Material Constituent Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "At a $62,392 income with 44.7-year median age, this constituency evaluates on whether government is delivering tangible relief from rising healthcare costs, property tax burden, and wage stagnation — not on abstract conservative principles. An incumbent or challenger who leads with ideological positioning rather than specific answers to cost-of-living pressures will find that rhetorical advantage evaporates quickly when constituents benchmark against their actual monthly bills. Unopposed victory can create complacency about this risk; the moment a credible challenger appears offering concrete economic answers, ideology-first messaging becomes a vulnerability." }
