          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Durable Margins", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Democratic result in 2024 reflects current advantage but does not indicate stable support in a district composed of working-class and renter households whose economic circumstances are constrained and whose partisan loyalty is conditional on material improvement. In Pennsylvania's political geography, working-class communities have demonstrated capacity to swing toward Republican challengers when they perceive incumbent Democrats as not delivering on economic concerns. The absence of Republican opposition in 2024 is not evidence that opposition cannot materialize; it is evidence that Mullins' current credibility with the constituency is sufficient to deter a challenger. That credibility is not automatic and must be sustained through active engagement and measurable results." },
          { name: "Assuming Abstract Democratic Messaging Resonates in a Working-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency at $64,363 income and 43.0 years median age evaluates representatives primarily on concrete economic results—not on abstract appeals to party identity or progressive values. Messaging that emphasizes partisan positioning or cultural issues rather than housing affordability, healthcare access, wage protection, and property tax fairness misaligns with the actual priorities of working-class households in economic constraint. In a district where Republican challengers could gain traction by offering economic solutions that Democrats have not delivered, generic Democratic framing is strategically weak; specific economic governance is necessary." }
