          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Wage Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $82,365 median income and 26.9% college attainment indicate a blue-collar-to-professional constituency that evaluates on concrete economic outcomes rather than partisan loyalty or abstract messaging. Healthcare affordability, wages keeping pace with housing and childcare costs, and neighborhood safety are the primary material concerns across racial and ethnic lines in a district where neither party has consolidated a durable majority. A representative who addresses these concerns with specificity and measurable results will consolidate working-class voters across the district; one who defaults to generic progressive or conservative positioning will find voters receptive to an alternative." },
          { name: "Demographic Shift Toward Non-White Plurality", tag: "careful", why: "The combined non-white population (approximately 46.74%) is nearly equal to the 53.26% white population, and demographic trends across Pennsylvania suggest this balance will shift further toward non-white plurality within two election cycles. Any candidate running in PA-HD-115 must understand that this is structurally a coalition district where Hispanic, Black, and Asian voters together will soon represent the numerical majority, and current governing must reflect that emerging reality. Taking any of these communities' support for granted while consolidating white voter margins is a strategically brittle approach that will break under pressure from a challenger who actively engages these constituencies." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result in a district with nearly half its population non-white, a 19.8% renter rate, and no clear consolidated coalition is not a secure seat — it is a competitive plurality that could swing in either direction with modest shifts in turnout or persuasion. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 53% represents a comfortable hold rather than a contested outcome that must be actively defended will find the margin eroding rapidly. The 47% opposition is the most important strategic fact about this district." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic Voter Loyalty Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic voters at 21.41% are the second-largest demographic group and a swing constituency in a narrowly divided district; assuming their support remains with the incumbent without specific, ongoing engagement on the issues that matter to this community — economic mobility, education, healthcare access, immigration nuance — is a guarantee of electoral vulnerability. A challenger who offers specific, credible positioning on Hispanic economic concerns and community investment will find this population persuadable, and the district's 53-47 margin will flip." }
