          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 56-44 Democratic margin reflects a constituency of upper-working-class and lower-middle-class voters ($67,364 income, 42.2 median age) who have made a Democratic choice but are not ideologically locked to the party. These households evaluate on whether government delivers tangible benefits on healthcare costs, employment stability, and household financial security — not on cultural or ideological positioning. A candidate who addresses healthcare affordability, prescription drug costs, and job protection with specificity while maintaining Democratic alignment on core issues will consolidate the existing base more effectively than one defaulting to culture-war or abstract economic messaging." },
          { name: "Renter Economic Vulnerability and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 32.8% renter rate means nearly one-third of the constituency lacks homeowner asset cushion and faces direct rent exposure in economic downturns. In a district where Democratic support is contingent on material household security, renter households are simultaneously core Democratic voters and economically vulnerable to any policy that increases housing costs or reduces rental supply. A candidate must engage renter concerns with specific policies on housing development incentives and rental regulation, recognizing that dismissing housing as a secondary issue risks demobilizing a significant share of the base." },
          { name: "Culture-War Framing on Education", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where two-thirds of adults have not completed a four-year degree and parental concern centers on post-secondary opportunity and employment pathways, national culture-war positioning on K-12 curricula is a distraction from the education issues that actually mobilize this constituency. A candidate who defaults to abstract culture-war language or treats education as a proxy for cultural grievance rather than economic opportunity will underperform among working-class and renter voters evaluating on material household concerns." },
          { name: "Taking the 56-44 Margin as a Stable Floor Without Consolidating the Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The 56-44 Democratic result reflects a constituency that has chosen Democratic alignment, but the combination of upper-working-class income, low college attainment, and one-third renting means the base is held by material delivery and household economic security — not by inherited partisan loyalty or cultural identity. Any candidate who focuses strategically on persuading Republicans in a district already 56% Democratic while assuming the working-class and renter base will turn out regardless of attention risks losing the marginal voters who determine the outcome: the households for whom Democratic support is contingent on specific delivery on jobs, healthcare, and education." }
