          { name: "Cost of Living & Renter Household Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 26.9% renter rate in a district with $72,845 median income and 42.97% Hispanic composition signals a substantial household population sensitive to rent increases, utility costs, and housing affordability — particularly in the Lehigh Valley where rents have risen faster than wages. Younger residents (36.9 median age) and renters evaluating whether they can afford to stay in the district are the swing voters in a seat where Democrats won 56% in 2024. A representative who leads with concrete housing policy, tenant protections, and cost-of-living relief — not abstract economic rhetoric — addresses the material concerns driving the current Democratic margin." },
          { name: "Education Access & Skills Training in a Low-College-Attainment District", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.3% college attainment, PA-HD-126 has substantially below-average educational attainment for Pennsylvania, and this gap is likely concentrated among Hispanic and working-class communities for whom four-year degree pathways are economically inaccessible. Community college funding, apprenticeship programs, and K-12 resource equity are the practical education issues that resonate here — but any candidate who frames this as a deficit problem rather than a resource problem will activate defensiveness rather than buy-in from communities that know their own capacities." },
          { name: "Framing Hispanic Community Concerns as 'Immigration' Rather Than 'Economic'", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Hispanic residents are 42.97% of the population and Democratic margins are 56-44, positioning Hispanic community concerns primarily through an immigration frame — rather than through economic mobility, workforce access, and cost of living — misreads both the constituency and the current electoral alignment. The 2024 Democratic win suggests the district's Hispanic voters are evaluating on economic security and whether government is serving them, not on immigration posture alone." },
          { name: "Treating the 44% Republican Opposition as a Stable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 Democratic result in Pennsylvania is a win, but it is not a supermajority — and in a district with 47.61% white residents, a 36.9 median age, and renter-heavy economics, the Republican 44% likely includes both older homeowners and working-class white voters evaluating on economic grounds, not inherited loyalty. Any representative who consolidates the Democratic base and ignores what generated the 44% opposition — whether specific local governance failures, cost-of-living frustration, or declining economic opportunity — is vulnerable to a credible challenger who speaks to those concerns with specificity." }
