          { name: "Working-Class Cost of Living & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The median household income of $69,290 combined with a 38.6% renter rate indicates a district where working families face real housing cost pressure — renters at this income level are typically spending 30-40% of income on housing alone, constraining resources for healthcare, childcare, and education. The 29.6% college attainment rate suggests limited access to degree pathways that would raise incomes, trapping many households in wage stagnation despite the district's relative prosperity compared to rural areas. Property tax fairness, rental regulation, and the viability of public transit as an affordable transportation alternative are concrete policy issues with direct household impact. The fact that McNeill ran unopposed in 2024 does not eliminate these material concerns — they will resurface if a challenger appears who directly addresses them." },
          { name: "Building Authentic Minority Community Relationships", tag: "careful", why: "With Hispanic at 24.37%, Black at 6.93%, and Asian at 1.97%, PA-HD-133 is a genuinely diverse urban district where minority communities represent over 35% of the population. The unopposed 2024 election result masks whether the incumbent has built durable relationships with these communities around their specific concerns or is benefiting from incumbent inertia and lack of opposition. Any challenger who can demonstrate deeper engagement with Hispanic economic concerns, Black community healthcare and education access, and immigrant community integration will find real electoral opportunity. The apparent Democratic dominance of the district should not obscure the fact that community-specific investment and responsiveness — not inherited partisan loyalty — is what sustains diverse urban coalitions in competitive statewide or federal races." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed Victory as Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 win is a data point about candidate recruitment and opposition party capacity in that cycle, not evidence of an unshakeable constituency. In a district with $69,290 income, 38.6% renters, and over 35% minority population, material economic concerns exist whether or not they are currently being articulated by an opposition candidate. Any incumbent who interprets an unopposed victory as permission to neglect constituent engagement, housing affordability work, or minority community relationship-building is creating the conditions for a credible challenger to emerge. The 2024 result is a win to build on with increased investment in constituent service and economic policy work, not a license to coast." },
          { name: "Appealing to Abstract Conservative Positioning on the District's Working-Class Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "With median income of $69,290, a 29.6% college attainment rate, and 38.6% renters, this is not a district of affluent professionals or comfortable retirees — it is a working and lower-middle-class urban constituency for whom wage growth, housing costs, and healthcare access are lived economic realities. Any messaging that emphasizes low taxes on the wealthy, reduced business regulation, or opposition to social services without offering concrete answers to why those positions serve working families' actual economic interests will fail to resonate or will activate opposition. The unopposed 2024 result creates no license to ignore the material conditions of a plurality of the district's households." }
