          { name: "Campaigning on Partisan Identity Rather Than Competence", tag: "avoid", why: "A college-educated, middle-income constituency at $84,616 with 33.8% college attainment evaluates primarily on whether government is competent and delivers on specific concerns — not on partisan emotional appeals or abstract ideological positioning. An incumbent who campaigns on party identity rather than on specific property tax management, education funding, and affordability achievements risks alienating the college-educated homeowner majority that forms the district's core. This constituency has the education and income to evaluate competing claims critically and will abandon a party or candidate perceived as ideologically rigid or incompetent." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Structural Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result in a district with a 73.69% white majority, middle-income profile ($84,616), and no overwhelming partisan demographic advantage is not a structural Democratic stronghold — it is a performance-based hold dependent on whether this specific incumbent is perceived as competent and responsive. Any Democratic candidate who campaigns as though 59% represents a safe base rather than as though every homeowner bloc and minority community required active engagement risks losing swing voters to a credible Republican alternative. The margin appears safe until it suddenly is not, and a 59% incumbent who takes this district for granted is vulnerable to a well-positioned challenger." }
