          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Management for Affluent Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.1 median age and $131,297 income, this district comprises employed professionals and established homeowners for whom healthcare access, insurance coverage, and pharmaceutical costs are material economic concerns. Unlike lower-income constituencies where healthcare is primarily a coverage and emergency-access issue, affluent professionals evaluate healthcare policy on quality, choice, and cost containment. A legislative agenda that addresses healthcare costs, insurance market competition, and prescription drug pricing with specificity demonstrates engagement with this constituency's actual material concerns." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization vs. Constituent Service Orientation", tag: "careful", why: "The 41% Democratic opposition in a wealthy, educated, professional-class district signals that genuine ideological disagreement or perception of poor governance exists within the Republican coalition. Whether this 41% is driven by social policy concerns, perception of tax fairness, education funding priorities, or belief that partisan positioning is overriding constituent service, it represents a meaningful swing constituency that neither party can take for granted. A representative perceived as prioritizing party-line votes over district-specific constituent outcomes risks losing this constituency to a challenger who can credibly claim to represent district interests above partisan positioning." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Unified or Safely Republican", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 margin in Pennsylvania is not a supermajority — it is a working majority in a district where 41% actively chose the opposition. The high education level (46.6% college) and high income ($131,297 median) of the constituency mean that the opposition is not concentrated among disadvantaged voters, but among peers of the incumbent — professionals and homeowners with equal resources and sophistication. Treating this district as ideologically settled rather than as a competitive constituency where voters evaluate on specific policy outcomes will allow a well-resourced challenger to consolidate the opposition and peel off swing voters dissatisfied with incumbent governance." },
          { name: "Populist Messaging or Anti-Institutional Rhetoric", tag: "avoid", why: "PA-HD-147's demographics — 46.6% college-educated, $131,297 median income, 43.1 median age, 79% homeownership — define a constituency whose economic interests depend on institutional stability: functioning schools, property-value protection, reliable infrastructure, and market confidence in local quality of life. Populist messaging that attacks institutions, suggests institutional incompetence, or implies that government cannot deliver will activate this constituency against the candidate, because these voters' material security depends on institutional competence. This district requires substantive governance narrative, not anti-institutional theater." }
