          { name: "Positioning Education Funding as a Partisan Issue Rather Than a Community Investment", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 75.8% of adults are college-educated and the median household income is $167,320, education funding is not perceived as a partisan issue — it is perceived as a question of whether the community's most important institution is receiving adequate resources to serve an educated, demanding parent constituency. Rhetoric that frames education funding as a partisan concern or that pitches education investment as a Democratic priority rather than a community necessity alienates the affluent, educated center of this constituency. The electoral winning message is that this district's schools deserve world-class funding because this is a world-class community, not because one party is more committed to education than another." }
