          { name: "Education Funding & School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with 55% college-educated adults and median age 42.1 — dominated by professionals and families with school-age children — education funding quality and school performance are foundational to property values and family retention. A Democratic representative who can demonstrate legislative progress on education adequacy, equitable funding formulas, and school accountability is building durable constituent support among the economic base most likely to vote. Conversely, any perception that Democratic education policy prioritizes redistribution over local school quality will create an opening for Republican messaging about fiscal responsibility." },
          { name: "Hispanic Workforce Integration & Visibility", tag: "careful", why: "The 12.82% Hispanic population in an affluent suburban district is concentrated disproportionately in service, construction, and healthcare-support sectors — economically present but often politically invisible in district-level messaging and constituent engagement. This is a community with real concerns about wage fairness, credential recognition, family healthcare access, and economic mobility that deserve specific legislative attention rather than assumption that broad Democratic positions automatically serve them. Engagement here can build loyalty among a growth-population demographic that Republican outreach is increasingly targeting." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as a Safe Majority in Affluent Suburbia", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent suburban voters are high-engagement, high-information constituents who evaluate candidates on concrete policy delivery and are more than capable of switching support if they conclude their representative is not delivering. The $139,529 median income and 55% college-educated rate mean this constituency has the resources and sophistication to support a primary challenge or vote for a credible Republican challenger who addresses their economic concerns. Any incumbent or candidate who treats 58% as a durable base rather than a contingent coalition that requires active constituent service will find the margin eroding rapidly." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization Over Fiscal Pragmatism", tag: "avoid", why: "This is a district of suburban professionals for whom fiscal competence, government efficiency, and delivery on promised outcomes matter more than partisan tribal identity. Any messaging that leans heavily on national partisan conflict or abstract ideological positioning — rather than demonstrating specific legislative achievement on education, property tax fairness, and healthcare cost management — will alienate the swing constituency most likely to decide this seat. Sappey's strategy must emphasize constituent service and concrete results, not partisan combat." }
