          "Lead with education and workforce development specificity: a 63.7% college-educated constituency in Pennsylvania's economy expects sophisticated engagement with how state policy supports talent retention, graduate workforce pipeline, and professional-class economic opportunity. Generic education rhetoric will not move this constituency; specific proposals on higher education funding, workforce training, and the connection between state education investment and professional-class job creation will.",
          "Address the 17.6% renter population and younger professional concerns explicitly: one in five households is renting in a district with $152,702 median income, signaling younger professionals or mobile workers whose housing costs, education debt, and cost-of-living concerns differ materially from the homeowner majority. This constituency evaluates on whether state government is managing costs and supporting economic mobility for young professionals, not on traditional homeowner property-tax anxiety.",
          "Engage the Asian American 8.34% and smaller Black and Hispanic populations with substantive constituent attention: in an 81% white district, minority communities can become invisible in political strategy despite representing nearly 15% of the population. Professional-class economic opportunity, healthcare access, and whether state policy supports diverse economic participation are the governance concerns that will activate or alienate these voters in a swing district where every bloc matters."
