          { name: "Homeowner Asset Protection & Tax Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 82.1% homeownership and $101,924 income, property value, school district quality, and tax burden are directly material to constituent net worth and financial planning. A Democratic incumbent who demonstrates concrete attentiveness to property tax fairness, school funding efficiency, and whether local government spending delivers proportional value for affluent households addresses the fiscal concerns that motivate the 42% Republican opposition even in educated, prosperous communities. Messaging that acknowledges these concerns — rather than dismissing them as parochial or unenlightened — provides credible Democratic positioning on fiscal responsibility." },
          { name: "Managing the 42% Republican Opposition in an Affluent, Educated District", tag: "careful", why: "A 58-42 margin in a district this economically secure and education-forward is not a landslide; it reflects a meaningful Republican constituency within a professional-class, college-educated population. This opposition is likely composed of small-business owners, finance professionals, and economically conservative voters who view Democratic regulation and tax positioning as burdensome — not working-class populists or culturally reactionary voters. Engaging this opposition requires respectful acknowledgment of fiscal and regulatory concerns, not partisan dismissal; a Democratic incumbent who appears to take these voters' concerns seriously can prevent erosion of the 58% margin, while one who treats them as illegitimate or ignorant risks consolidating opposition turnout." },
          { name: "Assuming Education and Income Demographics Guarantee Democratic Reelection", tag: "avoid", why: "The combination of 43% college attainment, $101,924 income, and 58% Democratic result can create an illusion of automatic reelection. In reality, affluent, educated constituencies are increasingly volatile because they evaluate candidates and policy on performance and competence rather than inherited partisan loyalty. A Democratic incumbent who assumes the demographic base is locked and neglects sustained constituent engagement, town halls, and specific attention to constituent concerns about school quality, tax burden, or government efficiency risks allowing a credible Republican challenger to mobilize the 42% opposition and potentially compete for swing voters. This is a seat that requires active stewardship, not demographic complacency." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Homogeneous", tag: "avoid", why: "The 58-42 result and the professional-class, college-educated profile can create a false impression that PA-HD-161 is uniformly progressive on cultural and social issues. In reality, educated, affluent constituencies often hold diverse views on cultural questions, with meaningful populations who are economically progressive but culturally conservative, or who prioritize fiscal discipline over social spending. A Democratic candidate who campaigns as though the district is uniformly aligned with progressive cultural positioning risks alienating swing voters and college-educated moderates who are economically Democratic but skeptical of expansive social spending or cultural progressivism. Messaging that emphasizes practical governance, fiscal responsibility, and education quality performs better with this constituency than abstract cultural appeals." }
