          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as a Mandate Rather Than Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election indicates that Republican opposition was organizationally weak, not that constituent satisfaction is universal or that the representative has earned a durable mandate. At $60,138 median income, 44.6% renters, and 27.2% college attainment, the constituency contains households under real economic pressure who are not voting Republican because they perceive the GOP as even worse — not because they are enthusiastically aligned with the incumbent. If economic conditions worsen or a credible challenger emerges who specifically addresses renter affordability, childcare costs, and wage stagnation, the current electoral margin may evaporate quickly." },
          { name: "Abstract Progressive Positioning Without Concrete Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A working-class renter-majority district at $60,138 median income evaluates representatives on whether they are materially improving household economic conditions — not on whether their rhetoric aligns with progressive values. Positioning on national partisan issues, abstract social justice framing, or policy proposals that do not connect to immediate rent, childcare, healthcare, and wage concerns can ring hollow in a constituency where households are managing real budget constraints. The unopposed result provides an opportunity to build concrete constituent relationships on material issues now, before electoral competition forces that accountability." }
