          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Permanent Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% margin in 2024 reflects the absence of a Democratic challenger, not durable constituent satisfaction across all population segments — it is a snapshot of one cycle, not a guarantee of future results. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though the unopposed result proves the district's safety will be unprepared when the next Democratic challenger identifies real governance gaps (healthcare access for renters, service gaps for older adults, neglect of the AIAN community) and organizes around them. The strategic task is not to manage a safe seat but to prevent it from becoming vulnerable." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 30.1% Renter Constituency as Fundamentally Different from Homeowners", tag: "avoid", why: "Renters at 30.1% of the district represent younger adults and working-class households whose governance concerns (eviction protection, housing affordability, access to transportation and childcare services) diverge significantly from the homeowner majority. Treating the renter constituency as a secondary or absorbed segment rather than as constituent with distinct legitimate interests creates an opening for a future challenger to organize renters around feeling overlooked. Even in a safe district, explicit attention to renter-specific issues prevents the creation of an opposition constituency waiting for its candidate." }
