          { name: "Workforce Development and Non-Degree Credentials", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 24.6% college attainment — among the lower rates in comparable districts — PA-HD-173's workforce is more reliant on apprenticeships, union training, vocational certification, and direct entry into working-class occupations than on four-year degree pathways. This is not a deficiency but a demographic fact: the district needs robust investment in community college partnerships, union apprenticeship programs, and employer-sponsored training that leads to family-sustaining wages. Any candidate who can articulate specific support for these pathways — or demonstrate a record of securing funding for them — addresses the material skill and credential pipeline that working-class households depend on for economic mobility." },
          { name: "Racial Equity in Economic Opportunity and Public Safety", tag: "careful", why: "At 19.67% Black and 15.14% Hispanic, PA-HD-173 has meaningful racial-minority constituencies whose economic mobility and experience of public safety are genuine governance concerns. In a plurality-white district, these communities' civic and economic needs can become peripheral to majority concerns — but the electoral consequences of overlooking them are real. Any incumbent or challenger who treats racial equity as a siloed issue rather than as integral to the district's broader working-class economic security narrative will find these constituencies either underengaged or actively supporting the opposition." },
          { name: "Running an Opponent-Less Campaign as an Incumbent", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 is a political asset that can calcify into complacency if not managed carefully. An incumbent who treats the unopposed result as validation that the seat is secure, rather than as evidence that the Republican Party deemed a challenge futile, risks being vulnerable to a well-funded challenger in the next cycle. The unopposed result should accelerate constituent engagement and delivery on economic commitments, not reduce it — because the reason no one ran was likely resource allocation, not demographic inevitability." },
          { name: "Treating the Seat as Safely Democratic Regardless of Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A working-class constituency at $66,190 income with 36.4% renters is not ideologically locked into either party; it is pragmatically evaluating whether government is improving material conditions. If a Democratic incumbent has not demonstrably increased wages, expanded affordable housing, or secured workforce training funding, the 'Democratic majority' in a 51% white, 35% Black-and-Hispanic district can shift if Republicans mount a credible economic message or if younger working-class voters simply abstain. Electoral margins in working-class districts are built on delivery, not assumption." }
