          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Permanent Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result may reflect long-term Democratic consolidation or temporary Republican disengagement; treating it as permanent is strategically dangerous. A district this educated and economically secure is capable of supporting a credible centrist or reform-oriented challenger if the incumbent is perceived as not delivering on material quality-of-life governance. The absence of opposition in one cycle is not a permission structure to neglect constituent service or assume voter loyalty is permanent." },
          { name: "Assuming High Income Automatically Means Satisfied Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "A $119,548 median household income does not mean constituents are satisfied with government delivery or that they lack material concerns requiring policy attention. Educated, economically secure urban professionals often have high expectations for service quality, policy sophistication, and alignment between stated values and governance outcomes — and they will organize against an incumbent perceived as underperforming on these dimensions. Income level correlates with voting capacity, not political satisfaction; a high-income district can be highly contested if governance fails to meet constituent expectations." }
