          { name: "Allowing Smaller Communities to Become Invisible Within the District's Demographic Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 81.74% Black majority can create an optical illusion that the district is monolithic, but the 18.26% non-Black population — Hispanic, White, Asian, and other communities — are real constituents with specific needs that become invisible if the representative treats the district as uniformly Black. In districts of this character, smaller communities become sources of political vulnerability when they perceive the representative as serving only the demographic majority; active engagement of these communities as integral constituencies, not afterthoughts, prevents both representational failure and future electoral risk." }
