          { name: "Homeowner Financial Stability & Infrastructure Investment", tag: "careful", why: "The 58.3% white plurality and 61.8% homeownership rate represent a substantial constituency with concrete economic interests in property tax fairness, school funding certainty, and infrastructure that serves both residential neighborhoods and property values. This is not a monolithic 'base' — homeowners are diverse in age, income, and partisan preference — but property-tax fairness and school-funding reliability are material concerns for households at $68,204 median income that must be addressed directly rather than displaced by renter-focused messaging. In a tied district, holding reasonable homeowner support while mobilizing renter turnout is the winning formula; neglecting either produces a vulnerable margin." },
          { name: "Treating 50-50 as a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50-50 partisan split is unstable equilibrium, not a durable majority or a safe opposition base. In a district this evenly divided, small shifts in turnout patterns — particularly among renters and non-white voters, who are less locked into inherited partisan loyalty — or in economic conditions affecting housing costs and job availability can flip the margin quickly. Any candidate or incumbent who campaigns as though 50-50 represents their consolidated base, rather than a perpetually contested outcome, is strategically blind to the actual competitive reality." },
          { name: "Single-Constituency Dominance in Campaign Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district split 50-50 where 38.2% are renters, 37-40% are non-white, and 61.8% are homeowners with diverse partisan preferences, no single constituency constitutes a winning coalition alone. A campaign that mobilizes renters while ignoring homeowner concerns, or that focuses exclusively on non-white communities while neglecting white working-class economic anxiety, or that defaults to homeowner tax priorities while dismissing renter affordability, will find the opposition mobilizes the neglected segment into a higher margin. This district requires simultaneous, specific engagement with multiple constituencies whose material concerns may differ." }
