          { name: "Education Quality & Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 46.1% college attainment and a 41.7-year median age centered on family-formation years, PA-SD-9 is a district where public school quality and education pathway accessibility are direct family concerns. School funding, teacher compensation, curriculum standards, and college-readiness outcomes are the material governance issues that will determine whether professional-class parents remain engaged with Democratic leadership or conclude that Republican candidates offer better stewardship. Education is the issue most directly connected to why these households live in this district and what they expect from their state representative." },
          { name: "Understanding the 44% Republican Opposition", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district this affluent and educated, the 44% Republican support is not a base of economically struggling voters but of professional-class households that have actively evaluated both candidates. These voters may be concerned about tax policy, regulatory burden on small business, school curriculum, development patterns, or ideological positioning — but they are not voting out of economic desperation. Any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat must understand with specificity which of these concerns drives the opposition's support and offer governance answers that take those concerns seriously rather than dismissing them as partisan noise." },
          { name: "Cost of Living & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 25.6% renter rate in a $104,305-median-income district indicates that a meaningful portion of the constituency is priced into housing at the edge of affordability — younger professionals, service-sector workers, and some minority communities for whom housing costs represent a significant budget constraint. Zoning policy, housing supply, and development approval are local and state policy levers that directly affect affordability; a candidate who engages this constituency's material housing concerns with specificity builds credibility with a swing group, but messaging that treats housing as an abstract justice issue without addressing supply constraints and development incentives will fall flat with pragmatic, educated swing voters." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture-War Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "PA-SD-9's professional-class, college-educated constituency evaluates candidates primarily on governance competence and specific policy solutions to material problems — not on abstract partisan identity or culture-war positioning. A candidate who leads with divisive social rhetoric rather than education quality, fiscal management, and community development will alienate the swing voters and minority communities whose engagement determines electoral performance in this district." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result is a working majority in an educated, affluent district where the opposition is not economically struggling but professionally accomplished and capable of making nuanced electoral judgments. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 56% represents a durable floor rather than a margin earned through specific constituent attention and demonstrated competence on the issues that matter to professional-class households and minority communities will find it vulnerable in the next competitive cycle." }
