          { name: "Healthcare Access & Institutional Trust", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 43.5 median age and $118,861 income profile a constituency that navigates both childcare/family healthcare and is beginning to approach concerns about long-term care and aging-parent support. Democratic positioning on healthcare expansion, insurance access, and institutional stability in healthcare delivery aligns with the material interests of this demographic. Demonstrating that Democratic governance actually delivers healthcare access improvements — not just rhetorical commitment — is how a Democratic incumbent deepens the 59% margin with the professional-class voters who currently support the party." },
          { name: "Fiscal Management & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "careful", why: "While the 59% Democratic result suggests college-educated voters prioritize education and environment over tax concerns, the $118,861 income and homeowner-dominant profile (79.1% non-renters) means property tax burden is a material household expense that cannot be ignored. If Democratic governance is perceived as fiscally reckless or if property taxes rise significantly faster than services improve, the affluent suburban constituency can shift — educated voters' support for progressive governance is conditional on belief that it is fiscally sustainable. Any Democratic incumbent must demonstrate that education and environmental investment are funded responsibly, not through tax increases that outpace middle-class income growth." },
          { name: "Treating the 59% as a Safe Landslide", tag: "avoid", why: "While 59% in a Biden-era suburban seat suggests structural Democratic strength, the college-educated constituency's evaluation is fundamentally performance-based and conditional. These voters chose Democratic governance because they believe it delivers on education, healthcare, and climate — not because of inherited party loyalty. If the incumbent fails to demonstrate tangible progress on any of those domains, or if fiscal mismanagement undermines confidence in competence, the 59% can erode quickly toward the 48-50% range where a credible Republican suburban professional could compete." },
          { name: "Conceding Fiscal Responsibility to Republicans", tag: "avoid", why: "The most dangerous strategic error for a Democratic incumbent in PA-SD-10 would be abandoning the fiscal-responsibility frame to Republicans and positioning Democrats as the high-spending party willing to burden affluent homeowners with tax increases. At $118,861 income and 79.1% homeownership, this constituency will not support progressive governance that comes at direct cost to their household finances. A Democratic incumbent must own the fiscal-responsibility argument — demonstrating that education and healthcare investment are funded through efficient government and economic growth, not through property tax burden shifting to the affluent suburban base." }
