          { name: "Partisan Ideology as a Primary Campaign Frame", tag: "avoid", why: "PA-SD-16 is not an ideologically driven district; it is a competence-driven district of affluent, educated voters who evaluate on stewardship, not partisan team loyalty. Campaigning this seat on abstract conservative or liberal positioning — rather than on specific plans for schools, infrastructure, and tax fairness — misses the actual basis on which these voters are making decisions. A candidate who defaults to national partisan messaging is ceding the local governance competence argument to a challenger who offers specifics." },
          { name: "Assuming Homeowner Majority Unity as a Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "While 80.6% homeownership is the district's dominant characteristic, affluent homeowners are not a monolithic voting bloc — they split 54-46 in the last election, indicating real disagreement on whether current governance is delivering. The college-educated majority (42%) makes individual evaluation more likely than bloc voting; the $105,856 income means voters can afford to support candidates across the partisan spectrum if they believe that candidate will manage their district's affairs more effectively. Treating homeowners as a secure base rather than as individual evaluators of competence is a path to losing them to a stronger governance narrative from a challenger." }
