          { name: "Treating the 59% Result as a Safe Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 33.6% renters, a growing Hispanic population, and educated white voters who are persuadable on competence grounds, 59% is not a safe baseline — it is the current margin of a constituency that is actively evaluating on performance and economic experience. Any Democratic candidate or incumbent who campaigns as though this result represents a secure hold, rather than one that must be defended through specific constituent engagement on housing, schools, economic opportunity, and government performance, is misreading the district's underlying dynamics and vulnerable to a credible challenger who addresses the material concerns this constituency shares." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency Invisibility in Housing Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "The 33.6% renter rate — representing roughly one-third of the district's voters — creates a direct tension with the property-owner majority, but renters in a $84,162 median income district are not economically marginal; they are professionals and families facing housing cost pressure even at upper-middle-class income levels. Any Democratic incumbent or challenger who frames housing policy exclusively around homeowner concerns or who allows renter affordability and tenant protection to become secondary priorities risks losing this one-third of the electorate to either abstention or an alternative candidate who takes housing justice seriously as an economic issue for middle-class renters." }
