          "Engage the 40% Republican opposition directly on fiscal and regulatory grounds: affluent, college-educated Republican voters in suburban/exurban districts typically oppose Democrats on tax and spending grounds, not cultural grounds. Understand whether this opposition is ideological or circumstantial, and whether it can be moved through specific fiscal governance commitments or alternative policy positioning.",
          "Activate the 27% non-white constituency (18.91% Black + 8.01% Asian, plus Hispanic) with issue-specific attention: healthcare access, economic mobility, educational equity, and criminal justice reform are the material concerns of these communities in a district with above-average income but potentially unequal distribution across racial lines. This is not a peripheral constituency—it is the difference between a 60% result and a potential majority.",
          "Treat the 60-40 margin as the current baseline, not the floor: in a district this affluent and educated, credible alternative candidates have room to compete if they can convince college-educated voters that their fiscal stewardship or regulatory approach better serves the district's economic interests. Active constituent engagement on material governance issues is essential to durability."
