          { name: "Assuming the 69-31 Margin is Durable Without Testing It", tag: "avoid", why: "Non-competitive electoral environments create structural incentive to neglect constituent concerns among the opposition and to assume base loyalty without demonstrating ongoing governance delivery. A 69-31 district in Pennsylvania is not guaranteed to remain 69-31 if demographic change accelerates or if the incumbent's record on working-class economic concerns becomes vulnerable to credible challenge. Treating the current margin as reflecting immutable partisan identity rather than ongoing constituent approval is the primary governance risk PA-SD-33's incumbent faces." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Service Documentation", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 69-31 environment, the temptation to rely on partisan messaging and base mobilization rather than building a documented record of constituent service and economic delivery is acute. If the margin ever begins to narrow — whether through demographic change, challenger mobilization, or constituent dissatisfaction with economic performance — a legislative record built primarily on abstract conservative positioning rather than specific constituent delivery becomes defensible only to those already convinced. The incumbent's best protection against future electoral vulnerability is a record of active constituent engagement and concrete economic delivery, not assumption that partisan loyalty alone will sustain a 69-31 margin indefinitely." }
